\title{Base R Filled Contour Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads a base R \code{filled.contour()} call as the contour it draws.

\code{filled.contour} draws the same level curves \code{contour()} does and fills the
bands between them, so it is read the same way: one curve per level, from
\code{grDevices::contourLines()}, which is the computation the drawing itself
runs. One chart with two spellings gets one reading.

Two things differ from \code{contour()}, and only two:

\strong{The level default.} \code{contour.default} takes \code{nlevels = 10} and
\code{filled.contour} takes \code{nlevels = 20}, and the number decides the whole
announced set through \code{pretty(zlim, nlevels)}. Everything else about
resolving the call -- the \verb{(x, y, z, ...)} slots, the
\code{if (missing(z)) z <- x} fallback, the \code{list(x =, y =, z =)} unpacking,
the \code{zlim} default -- is identical in both, so this class overrides the
number and inherits the rest.

\strong{The chart cannot be highlighted, and the inherited code already knows
it.} \code{contour()} writes one \code{lines} grob per curve, which is what
\code{generate_selectors()} pairs against. \code{filled.contour} writes one
\code{polygon} grob for the entire field, and gridSVG exports it as a flat run
of pieces. Measured on a 6x5 grid drawn at the 17 default levels:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{grobs written    graphics-plot-2-filled-contour-1   (one)
SVG polygons     graphics-plot-2-filled-contour-1.1.1 .. .1.160
curves announced 40
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

160 pieces against 40 curves, and against 17 levels: the polygons are the
grid's cells cut by the level crossings, not the bands and not the curves.
Nothing pairs, so nothing is emitted -- the inherited
\code{generate_selectors()} finds no \code{-contour-N-N} grob, its count check fails,
and it withholds the list, which is the same answer it gives a \code{contour()}
whose grobs and curves disagree. A layer with no selectors is announced,
sonified and navigated; it is the visual highlight alone that is missing,
and that is the established degradation here (#89) rather than a reason to
ship a picture.

Note also that the field is drawn in the \strong{second} plot region: the call
lays out a colour key as \code{graphics-plot-1} and the field as
\code{graphics-plot-2}. Nothing here depends on that, because nothing here
addresses a grob, but a later attempt to highlight this chart will.

py-maidr declines the equivalent call. Its reason -- recorded in
\code{maidr/patch/contour.py} -- is that \code{contourf} hands back the filled paths
and "an outline of one runs along two different level curves", which is a
statement about deriving curves from what was drawn. It does not apply
here: R hands over \code{contourLines()}, so the curves announced are the level
curves themselves rather than an inference from the fill, and every one of
them is on the page as the boundary between two bands.
}
